A vector works well as a stack here. A collision is possible only when the last survivor is positive and the new asteroid is negative. The loop keeps removing smaller right-moving asteroids until the current one is gone or no collision remains.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> asteroidCollision(vector<int> &asteroids){
        vector<int> survivors;

        for (int asteroid : asteroids) {
            bool alive = true;

            while (alive && !survivors.empty() && survivors.back() > 0 && asteroid < 0) {
                long long leftSize = survivors.back();
                long long rightSize = -(long long)asteroid;

                if (leftSize < rightSize) {
                    survivors.pop_back();
                } else if (leftSize == rightSize) {
                    survivors.pop_back();
                    alive = false;
                } else {
                    alive = false;
                }
            }

            if (alive) {
                survivors.push_back(asteroid);
            }
        }

        return survivors;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[10, 2, -5]`: `10` and `2` are stored first. Then `-5` removes `2`, but it cannot remove `10`, so the result is `[10]`.

An empty input, all asteroids moving in one direction, and equal sizes such as `[8, -8]` are handled without special code.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because every asteroid is added and removed at most once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the survivor vector.

This keeps the surviving asteroids at the beginning of the input array. The variable `size` acts like the stack length. When a negative asteroid arrives, it checks the last stored positive asteroid and moves backward if that positive asteroid is destroyed.

The input array is changed while the method runs. Only the first `size` positions are returned in the new result array.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] asteroidCollision(int[] asteroids) {
        int size = 0;

        for (int current : asteroids) {
            boolean alive = true;

            while (alive && size > 0 && asteroids[size - 1] > 0 && current < 0) {
                long leftSize = asteroids[size - 1];
                long currentSize = -(long) current;

                if (leftSize < currentSize) {
                    size--;
                } else if (leftSize == currentSize) {
                    size--;
                    alive = false;
                } else {
                    alive = false;
                }
            }

            if (alive) {
                asteroids[size++] = current;
            }
        }

        int[] answer = new int[size];
        System.arraycopy(asteroids, 0, answer, 0, size);
        return answer;
    }
}
```

The equal case removes the stored asteroid and does not write the current one. If the current asteroid is smaller, it simply disappears and the stored data stays unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned array, with `O(1)` extra working space besides it.

A normal array can represent the stack in JavaScript. Positive asteroids are added directly. For a negative asteroid, the `while` loop compares it with the latest survivor only when that survivor is moving right.

The `alive` flag makes the equal-size and losing cases easy to separate. An empty array, negative asteroids at the beginning, and pairs moving away from each other need no special handling.

```javascript
class Solution {
    asteroidCollision(asteroids) {
        const survivors = [];

        for (const asteroid of asteroids) {
            let alive = true;

            while (
                alive &&
                survivors.length > 0 &&
                survivors[survivors.length - 1] > 0 &&
                asteroid < 0
            ) {
                const last = survivors[survivors.length - 1];
                const currentSize = -asteroid;

                if (last < currentSize) {
                    survivors.pop();
                } else if (last === currentSize) {
                    survivors.pop();
                    alive = false;
                } else {
                    alive = false;
                }
            }

            if (alive) {
                survivors.push(asteroid);
            }
        }

        return survivors;
    }
}
```

Only a positive value followed by a negative value can collide. A negative value followed by a positive value is already moving apart, so both stay in the list.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the survivor array.

The input slice is reused as the stack. `top` tells where the last survivor is stored. When a larger incoming negative asteroid removes a positive one, `top` moves left and the same incoming value is checked again.

Dry run with `[4, 7, -10]`: `-10` first removes `7`, then checks `4` and removes it too. The slice ends with `[-10]`.

The returned slice points to the original input storage, so this method changes the input slice contents.

```go
func asteroidCollision(asteroids []int) []int {
    top := 0

    for _, current := range asteroids {
        alive := true

        for alive && top > 0 && asteroids[top-1] > 0 && current < 0 {
            previous := asteroids[top-1]

            if previous < -current {
                top--
            } else if previous == -current {
                top--
                alive = false
            } else {
                alive = false
            }
        }

        if alive {
            asteroids[top] = current
            top++
        }
    }

    return asteroids[:top]
}
```

The empty input returns an empty slice. Equal asteroids remove each other, and negative asteroids that have no positive survivor before them are stored normally.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each stored asteroid can be removed only once.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the returned slice.

